  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 144 AM MDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nWhile the main trough will eject northwest into the northern Rockies \nThursday into Friday, troughing associated with the sub-tropical jet \nwill persist over the desert southwest Friday into the weekend, \nkeeping moist southwest flow over New Mexico. The main area of \ndiffluence aloft will focus over eastern NM on Friday afternoon. A \nswath of MU CAPE of 500-700 J/kg will likely extend from south-\ncentral NM into the northeast corner of the state. Training of \nstorms could produce some localized flash flooding and the Marginal \nERO has been maintained with very little change. Given that elevated \ninstability will be driving much of this convection, high rain \nchances will continue into the overnight hours.\n\nThis second storm system will be more neutrally titled, allowing \nstronger southeasterly moisture advection into eastern NM over the \nweekend. Upslope flow along with strong diffluence aloft will drive \nprecipitation, but persistent low clouds could impact sfc based \ninstability. 48-hour rainfall totals continue to trend higher, with \n50th percentile amounts now showing a swath of 2-3\" in the east-\ncentral and northeast plains. High-end guidance (90th percentile) \nshows values upwards of 4.5\" for the same period. Drier westerly \nflow should eventually take over Monday or Tuesday of next week, \nwith only lingering PoPs across southeast New Mexico.",
